# vehje agent instructions

## Identity

Vehje is a general-purpose scripting language for game modding and
embeddable runtimes. Game-agnostic core: compiler + runtime + language
spec. Anything that names a specific game belongs in a sibling
extension repo, not here.

## Two-language strategy

**Rust compiler + Zig runtime, joined by a C ABI defined and validated
in Rust.**

- **Compiler** (Rust): lex, parse, resolve, typecheck, transpile,
  codegen, pass scheduler. Runs at dev time. Ships as a single static
  binary per target platform. Cross-compilation is a first-class
  concern.
- **Runtime** (Zig): executes macros, scratch variables, generative
  pipelines. Small, fast, embeddable. Speaks C ABI. Pulls no `std`,
  no Rust runtime.
- **Build tooling / deploy / orchestration**: written in Vehje
  itself once the language is usable (rustc/cargo pattern. the
  compiler does one thing; tooling runs on top of what the compiler
  emits).

## Crates

`mock/crates/` is intentionally empty at seed. The compiler crate
split is decided by the first four design rounds (R1–R4). Provisional
targets, not binding:

- `vehje-lex`. tokenisation
- `vehje-syntax`. parser + AST
- `vehje-resolve`. module resolution, use-tree, visibility
- `vehje-typecheck`. coherence, bind targets, generics, validator framework
- `vehje-schedule`. pass DAG (consumes `arvo-graph`)
- `vehje-codegen`. target-agnostic codegen
- `vehje`. top-level binary + orchestration

Until R1–R4 lands, crate-level rules and lints are stubs. The
framework is set; the contents arrive with the crates.

## Foundations

This repo consumes two sibling foundation repos:

- [`arvo`](https://github.com/orgrinrt/arvo). numeric primitives +
  graph / sparse / bitmask / spectral / comb algorithms. Used for the
  pass DAG (`arvo-graph`) and artifact read/write sets
  (`arvo-bitmask`).
- [`hilavitkutin`](https://github.com/orgrinrt/hilavitkutin).
  Pipeline execution engine. Used by the **runtime** for morsel-driven
  macro / scratch / generative execution. The compiler itself does
  single-shot DAG walks and rarely needs the frame runtime.

Reuse arvo and hilavitkutin. Do not reimplement DAG topology, bitmask
ops, sparse storage, or pipeline scheduling in Vehje.

## Extension pattern

Game-specific targets ship as **separate repos** that extend Vehje:

- `vehje-jomini` (future). Clausewitz (Stellaris / CK3 / HOI4 /
  Vic3 / EU4) grammar, bind targets, codegen to Clausewitz script.
- `vehje-w3` (future). Witcher 3 target.
- `vehje-ts4` (future). Sims 4 target.
- `vehje-lua` (future). generic Lua target.

The extension-point contract is one of the early design rounds. Until
then, do not inline game-specific vocabulary, types, or codegen into
this repo. it makes the core extraction harder later.

## Parity oracle

A Python prototype of Vehje lives at
`~/Dev/stellar-heritage/tools/vehje/` (~15k lines, 1500+ tests). It
is the **parity oracle** during the Rust port. run its test corpus
against the Rust implementation to verify semantic equivalence.

It is **not** a line-by-line translation source. The Rust shape
differs: tagged enums over Protocol duck-typing, monomorphised trait
dispatch over `isinstance` checks, `Result` over exceptions, static
composition over runtime registry lookups. Read the Python for
semantics; author the Rust from the design docs.

## Compiler posture

Strict by default, across both the Vehje language and the Rust
compiler implementation:

- Deny warnings at crate roots: `unused`, `unreachable_code`,
  `unused_must_use`, `unused_imports`, `dead_code`.
- No `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` outside tests and documented infallible
  paths.
- Tagged enum + trait dispatch; no `dyn Trait` / `TypeId` / `std::any`
  in framework code. Compile-time dispatch wins.
- Descriptive error variants. Errors carry context (span, phase,
  expected/actual), not just a string.
- Procedural macros only for Vehje language semantics. `macro_rules!`
  is acceptable internally in the Rust compiler implementation for
  boilerplate reduction, but never for Vehje itself. macros in the
  language are proc-only, scheduler-pass-driven, and typechecked.

## Single-binary distribution

End users should not need Python, Deno, .NET, JVM, or any runtime
alongside Vehje. `cargo build --release` produces one static binary
per target. No dynamic runtime deps. No plugin shared-object loading
at the compiler layer (the runtime may embed differently).

## Commit style

See `skills/commit-style/`. Short version: `type: lowercase imperative
message`, no parens, no trailing period, under 72 characters.

## What not to do

- Do not edit `.claude/` or `.github/` output. Those are generated
  from `mock/agent/*.tmpl` by `cargo mock`. Edit the template.
- Do not inline game-specific vocabulary (`clausewitz`, `witcher`,
  `stellaris`, `paradox`, …) into compiler or runtime code. Use an
  extension repo.
- Do not reimplement graph / DAG / bitmask / sparse / pipeline types.
  Use `arvo` and `hilavitkutin`.
- Do not translate the Python prototype line-by-line. Use it as a
  parity oracle, not a template.
- Do not bypass the design-round state machine by renaming changelist
  files manually. Use `cargo mock lock / unlock / deprecate / close`.
- Do not add crate-specific lints or hooks before R1–R4 decides the
  crate split. Document intent in the skill or framework doc; author
  the enforcement once crates exist.
